# Livewall — configuration
#
# Copy to `.env` and edit. Every value has a working default, so a bare clone
# runs without this file at all.
#
#   cp .env.example .env

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- backend
# An invalid (non-numeric) value for any of the *_TTL / *_BOARDS knobs falls
# back to its default and logs a warning, rather than being silently ignored.
#
# Bind address for a BARE-METAL run. Defaults to loopback: exposing a
# dashboard to the network should be a deliberate act, not an accident.
#
# ⚠ IGNORED under docker-compose. The compose file hardcodes 0.0.0.0 for the
#   container, because a process bound to 127.0.0.1 inside a container is
#   unreachable from the host even with a published port — Docker forwards to
#   the container's network interface, not its loopback. That is why the
#   compose value is a literal and not ${LIVEWALL_HOST}: copying this file
#   would otherwise put 127.0.0.1 in the container and silently break it.
#   Set this only when running the backend directly on the host.
LIVEWALL_HOST=127.0.0.1

# Port the backend listens on. Bare metal: the listen port.
# Under docker-compose this is the HOST-side port only — the container always
# listens on 8470, and compose publishes ${LIVEWALL_PORT:-8470}:8470.
LIVEWALL_PORT=8470

# How long a FINISHED board (status done/error) survives after its last update.
LIVEWALL_DEFAULT_TTL=1800

# How long a RUNNING board survives without an update (a crashed agent).
LIVEWALL_STALE_TTL=3600

# Hard cap on boards; the oldest-updated are evicted past it.
LIVEWALL_MAX_BOARDS=12

# CEILING for the runtime `max_boards` knob: PUT /api/config (or MCP set_config)
# is REFUSED above this, so a huge value cannot disable eviction and let boards
# accumulate without bound. Raise it deliberately if you need more than the
# default. An `LIVEWALL_MAX_BOARDS` above this at boot is clamped to it (logged).
LIVEWALL_MAX_BOARDS_LIMIT=1000

# Maximum bytes read from a single request body. A body over this is refused
# with 413: an oversize Content-Length is rejected up front, and a chunked body
# is capped while streaming. Keeps one huge POST from exhausting memory on a
# service that is open by default (and token-gated when LIVEWALL_TOKEN is set).
# Default 1048576 (1 MiB).
# LIVEWALL_MAX_BODY_BYTES=1048576

# Maximum concurrent SSE subscribers (GET /api/stream). Each holds a bounded
# queue; the next connection is refused with 503 and the bundled client
# retries. Default 100.
# LIVEWALL_MAX_SUBSCRIBERS=100

# Where runtime config changes persist, so a restart does not revert them.
# A write failure is surfaced (`PUT /api/config` -> 500, never a false ok), and
# a corrupt/unreadable file is logged and falls back to the defaults below.
# LIVEWALL_CONFIG_FILE=./backend/config.json

# Seconds of SSE silence before a keepalive snapshot is sent (keeps proxies
# from closing an idle stream, and gives the client fresh data so a quiet wall
# never looks stale). Default 15.
# LIVEWALL_KEEPALIVE=15

# Built client to serve at `/`. Defaults to ./web/dist next to the backend.
# LIVEWALL_WEB_DIR=./web/dist

# Seconds uvicorn waits for in-flight requests on shutdown before cancelling
# them; open SSE streams are cut at this bound. Default 3. Keep it well under
# your unit's TimeoutStopSec, or systemd will SIGKILL the process.
# LIVEWALL_GRACEFUL_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT=3

# uvicorn log level: critical | error | warning | info | debug
LIVEWALL_LOG_LEVEL=warning

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- auth
# Bearer token. UNSET (or empty) = the API is open, which is the quickstart
# default: `docker compose up` and a working board. SET = every /api/* endpoint
# requires it, as `Authorization: Bearer <token>` or `?token=<token>`. /health
# and the static client stay open, so liveness probes and the browser bundle
# need no credential.
#
# Set a long random value before exposing the port beyond loopback:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# WHERE IT IS READ FROM — a value in this file is NOT read by the backend or
# the CLI; they read only the process environment. So for a bare-metal run
# export it before starting the process:
#   export LIVEWALL_TOKEN=$(openssl rand -hex 32)
#   .venv/bin/python backend/app.py
# The MCP server reads this `.env` automatically (see below), and docker
# compose substitutes it from this file into the container. The browser client
# never reads an env var: it takes the token from `?token=<token>` once, stores
# it in localStorage, and sends it as a header.
# LIVEWALL_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- client (dev)
# Where the Vite dev server proxies /api to.
LIVEWALL_BACKEND=http://127.0.0.1:8470

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- mcp
# Base URL the MCP server talks to. Usually the same as above.
LIVEWALL_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8470
LIVEWALL_TIMEOUT=15

# The MCP server sends the token above (LIVEWALL_TOKEN) as a bearer header when
# it is set, so a token-protected backend needs no extra MCP configuration.

# Env file the MCP server loads, if present. Defaults to this repo-root `.env`.
# Set it in the ACTUAL environment, not in this file: `.env` is the file being
# loaded, so a value written here is read too late to change which file opens.
# (See docs/mcp.md.)
# LIVEWALL_MCP_ENV_FILE=./.env
